INT. OLD LIBRARY - RARE BOOKS ROOM - EVENING

A tall room of dark oak and leather. A ladder on rails. A reading table under a green lamp.

PROFESSOR HADLEIGH VANCE-OKONJO (50s, tweed, pipe unlit) examines a bound volume under magnification. Across the table, his assistant MISS PRISCILLA TREVELYAN-ADE (30s, cardigan, pencil behind her ear) records notes. In the corner, the librarian MR. OSWIN CHAMBERLAIN-BELL (70s, bow tie, white gloves) dusts a shelf.

HADLEIGH
Priscilla, look at this.

PRISCILLA
At what, Professor?

HADLEIGH
The binding. It has been recently altered.

PRISCILLA
Altered how?

HADLEIGH
The stitching. Someone has resewn the spine.

OSWIN
(from the corner)
That is impossible. No one has touched that volume in forty years.

HADLEIGH
Forty-one, Mr. Chamberlain-Bell, according to your register.

OSWIN
Then forty-one.

PRISCILLA
Professor, there's a name here on the flyleaf.

HADLEIGH
Read it.

PRISCILLA
"Cornelius Ambrose Wren."

OSWIN
Wren?

HADLEIGH
You know the name.

OSWIN
He was a donor. Long dead.

HADLEIGH
He died in nineteen thirty-two.

OSWIN
Quite.

HADLEIGH
Then who has been writing in his book?

PRISCILLA
There's fresh ink in the margins.

OSWIN
That cannot be.

HADLEIGH
Here, Priscilla. Hold the lamp lower.

PRISCILLA
The ink is still wet.

OSWIN
(sharply)
Put it down.

HADLEIGH
Mr. Chamberlain-Bell.

OSWIN
Put it down at once.

PRISCILLA
^What is it?

HADLEIGH
^What do you know?

Oswin crosses the room with surprising speed, takes the volume in both gloved hands, and clasps it to his chest.

OSWIN
I know that some books are not meant to be read.

HADLEIGH
By whom?

OSWIN
By anyone.

PRISCILLA
Mr. Chamberlain-Bell, you wrote in it.

OSWIN
(very quietly)
No, my dear. I have only been keeping it dry.

He lays the book on the table, opens it to a page, and steps back. In the green lamplight, a line of handwriting slowly begins to appear on the paper, letter by letter.

Hadleigh and Priscilla lean in together.
